# TIA Portal projects — never commit; exports live in simatic-ml/
*.ap20
*.ap[0-9][0-9]
*.zap[0-9][0-9]
scratch/
# ...and any SIBLING a lane invents. Measured 2026-08-14: a lane created
# `scratch-lane-slots/` at the repo root, untracked and UNIGNORED, while `scratch/`
# beside it was covered. A pattern protects the directory somebody thought of; this
# one covers the ones nobody did.
scratch-*/
*.plf
*.backup

# Real site scratch-copy projects (Amber-tier, docs/13-data-boundary.md) — never commit,
# not just the .apNN (Logs/, .backup, etc. aren't covered by the patterns above)
JOB9002 - Tom White Waste - Scratch Copy/

# The reference TIA project itself (S1 item 1) — same reasoning: never commit the live TIA
# project folder, only the sanitized exports it produces (simatic-ml/reference/, ir/reference/)
SampleProject/

# S6 generation sandbox (2026-07-15) — a live, working TIA project used to incrementally build up
# a real generated project via small prompted steps. Same reasoning as SampleProject/: never commit
# the live project folder itself, only whatever gets extracted from it. Anchored to repo root
# (2026-07-16) so the extracted corpus ir/test-project001/ + simatic-ml/test-project001/ CAN be
# committed: Green-tier by construction (docs/13-data-boundary.md, 2026-07-15 GenProject1 entry —
# codenamed test-project001 from 2026-07-17), kept as the durable S6 output and the standing
# validation corpus for reviewer skills (docs/15). The live TIA project folder itself keeps its
# original on-disk name (GenProject1/) for Openness continuity — see CLAUDE.md's codename note.
/GenProject1/

# Live runs — live engineering jobs worked end-to-end (docs/13-data-boundary.md, "Live runs" section,
# 2026-08-04). The AI has full working access to everything in here, Red-tier included; NOTHING in
# here is ever committed, and nothing verbatim from it is written into a committed doc, skill,
# pattern, memory or note. Covers the whole tree: specs, TIA projects, exports, working notes,
# correspondence — every file type, at every depth, no exceptions carved out below this line.
#
# Deliberately UNANCHORED and spelling-tolerant (2026-08-05): the root `Live Runs/` is where jobs
# actually live, but a live-run folder created at any depth (e.g. a job's own nested working copy,
# or an exports staging dir someone drops under scratch/) must be ignored too. A live-run leak is
# unrecoverable once pushed — the cost of a few redundant patterns is nothing next to that.
Live Runs/
LiveRuns/
Live-Runs/
live_runs/

# LIVE-RUN CONTENT THAT ESCAPED THE FOLDER (2026-08-08). The patterns above protect the DIRECTORY;
# they do nothing about a file carrying live-run content written somewhere else in the tree. That
# happened: an agent doing a tag sweep left two scratch files full of real production tag names in the
# REPO ROOT, untracked and NOT ignored — one `git add -A` from being committed, and several were run
# that day. Caught by the agent declaring them in its own report, not by any rule.
#
# So: ignore anything named for a job, at any depth. Job folders are named `J####` by convention, so
# a scratch file derived from one almost always carries the number. This is a backstop for an
# accident, not a licence to write live-run content outside its folder — that is still forbidden.
# A leak is unrecoverable once pushed; redundant patterns cost nothing.
J[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]*
j[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]*

# Never commit Siemens DLLs — reference Siemens.Engineering.dll by install path
Siemens.Engineering*.dll

# Sanitization mapping files (real name -> invented name) — never commit; the point of a
# sanitized artifact is that nothing links it back to the real source, including this
sanitization/

# Real site reference documents (Amber-tier, docs/13-data-boundary.md) — never commit. The
# real "Kestrel Shredder Systems"/JOB9003-K150 spec + functional description used to inform GenProject1's
# own (invented) functional design; GenProject1 itself stays Green-tier by never using these
# real names directly (see sanitization/Kestrel Shredder Systems.map.json).
SpecSheet.xlsx
FuncDesc.docx

# HMI alarm-import workbook (Amber-tier, docs/13-data-boundary.md 2026-07-29) — real JOB9003 alarm
# text, equipment names and trigger tags. Engineering output for a real job, local-only.
HMIAlarms.xlsx*

# OS / editor
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Claude Code local-only state — machine/session-specific, not shared project content
# (.claude/skills/ and other shared config are NOT covered by these and should still be committed)
.claude/settings.local.json
# ...AND ANY BACKUP OR TEMP BESIDE IT. Measured 2026-08-13: a `.bak` written by an agent mid-edit
# sat UNTRACKED-BUT-NOT-IGNORED while carrying a Live Runs project path (identifying name in the
# allowlisted download-probe invocation). The file itself is ignored; a sibling was not, so one
# `git add -A` would have committed a restricted identifier. The discipline "never git add -A" is
# real and is briefed to every lane — but a rule enforced only by discipline is the thing this
# repo keeps re-learning to make structural instead.
.claude/settings.local.json.*
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock

# S6 gen-block-modify-purpose validation: MotorVSDSystem answer key is a quarantined blind-
# isolation ground truth (Green/sanitized, docs/13 2026-07-20). Never commit it - the blind
# generator must never be able to discover it in the tree.
gen/_validation/MotorVSDSystem-purpose/answerkey/

# The test-environment project the owner gave the tooling outright (2026-08-14). A TIA project
# folder, ignored for the same reason as GenProject1/ and SampleProject/ above: it is a live
# Openness-managed workspace, not source. It was UNTRACKED AND UNIGNORED for several hours, which
# is one `git add -A` from committing it - the same shape as the stray settings .bak found on
# 2026-08-13.
/Test Enviroment/
